[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Background Jobs with Celery in Flask"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Web applications often need to execute time-consuming tasks like sending emails, processing uploaded files, or generating reports. Performing these tasks synchronously blocks the HTTP request-response cycle, leading to poor user experience. Celery is a distributed task queue that allows you to offload these tasks to background workers."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Prerequisites and Architecture"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"To implement Celery in a Flask application, you need three main components: Flask (the web framework), Celery (the task queue), and a message broker (such as Redis or RabbitMQ) to pass messages between Flask and the Celery workers."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Flask: Handles the incoming HTTP requests and triggers background tasks."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Redis: Acts as the message broker, storing the queue of tasks."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Celery Worker: A separate process that listens to the broker and executes the background tasks."},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Installation"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Install the required packages using pip. Make sure you also have Redis installed and running locally on your machine."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"pip install flask celery redis"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring Celery with Flask"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"To make Celery work seamlessly within the Flask application context, we need to create a helper function that initializes the Celery app using Flask's configuration."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"from celery import Celery
from flask import Flask

def make_celery(app):
    celery = Celery(
        app.import_name,
        backend=app.config["CELERY_RESULT_BACKEND"],
        broker=app.config["CELERY_BROKER_URL"]
    )
    celery.conf.update(app.config)

    class ContextTask(celery.Task):
        def __call__(self, *args, **kwargs):
            with app.app_context():
                return self.run(*args, **kwargs)

    celery.Task = ContextTask
    return celery

app = Flask(__name__)
app.config.update(
    CELERY_BROKER_URL="redis://localhost:6379/0",
    CELERY_RESULT_BACKEND="redis://localhost:6379/0"
)
celery = make_celery(app)"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"Defining Background Tasks"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once Celery is configured, you can define asynchronous tasks using the @celery.task decorator. Here is an example of a long-running task that simulates data processing."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"import time

@celery.task(name="tasks.process_data")
def process_data(duration):
    time.sleep(duration)
    return "Task completed successfully!""},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Triggering Tasks from Flask Routes"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"In your Flask route, instead of calling the function directly, use the delay() method. This pushes the task to the Redis queue and immediately returns a task ID to the client without waiting for completion."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"@app.route("/start-task", methods=["POST"])
def start_task():
    task = process_data.delay(5)
    return {"task_id": task.id, "status": "Task started"}, 202"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Checking Task Status"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can create another route to check the status and retrieve the result of a running or completed background task using its ID."},{"order":22,"type":"code","text":"from celery.result import AsyncResult

@app.route("/task-status/<task_id>", methods=["GET"])
def task_status(task_id):
    task_result = AsyncResult(task_id, app=celery)
    if task_result.ready():
        return {"status": task_result.status, "result": task_result.result}
    return {"status": task_result.status}"},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Running the Application and Worker"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"To run your system, you need to start both the Redis server, the Flask development server, and the Celery worker process in separate terminal windows."},{"order":25,"type":"code","text":"celery -A app.celery worker --loglevel=info"}]
